Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Counting to Seven While the Fence Bleeds
- Story & Context: In the summer of 2006, sixteen-year-old Toby Chen invented a dare game along the perimeter of the Port Mercer rail yard, forcing us to hold the rusted chain-link fence while counting the seconds between lightning flashes to guess when the pirate station would hijack our Walkman radios. When the sky ruptured and the ungrounded tower began transmitting, Toby took a stray surge through his palm that left his fingertips smelling of sulfur and wet copper, yet he collapsed into the marsh laughing as warped steel drums tore through the headphones and the voice inside the radio begged, "run till the iron counts down to your bones." The recording captured that night became the foundational anthem of the entire scene, preserving Toby’s breathless countdown beneath howling psych-rock distortion.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric album cover showing a rusted chain-link fence running through overgrown marsh reeds during a torrential thunderstorm, electrical sparks dancing off wet metal links under a violet-black sky, low-angle shot, film grain, melancholic 2000s indie sleaze aesthetic with cinematic lightning flash.

Track 2: Who Told You the Ground Wire Was Safe?
- Story & Context: Recorded during a Category 2 squall inside the radar shack, this track serves as Miri Vance's raw confession about stealing her late brother’s tropical steel drums from their mother's flooded basement and drowning them in fuzz boxes to contact his missing VHF boating frequency. Julian Ray left a single condenser microphone dangling from the zinc ceiling while hail battered the roof like shrapnel, capturing Miri breaking into weeping fits between bowing the steel notes as Kaelen Voss coaxed agonizing, backward tape loops from an overheating amplifier. Midway through the take, Miri choked on the sulfur fumes of a blown capacitor and cried out through the delay-soaked room, "I only tune the pans when the thunder makes me shake."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Poetic lyric-video visual depicting a dim, water-damaged coastal shack illuminated only by the green glow of vintage audio gear and flashes of lightning through broken windows, water dripping onto a tarnished steel drum, floating dust and rain mist, moody Kodachrome color palette.

Track 3: Cora’s Last Broadcast from Mercer Tower
- Story & Context: By late 2009, local authorities finally raided the Mercer marshlands after Station 89.1 broadcast continuously through a three-day supercell, only to find the transmitter shack entirely submerged in silt and the mysterious midnight operator, known only as Cora, completely gone. The search crew pulled up nothing but a fried zinc antenna and a mud-caked four-track recorder containing a final, looping drone where bowed steel pans chimed like church bells under a collapsing wall of fuzz guitar. Teenagers still gather on the breakwater during August squalls to watch the empty tower sway, listening to the crackling open frequency where a looped voice whispers endlessly into the downpour, "the sky forgot our names but kept the melody."
